nA + B + C = 18\n\nWe are also given that four years ago, Chris was the same age as Amy is now. This can be written as:\n\nC - 4 = A\n\nWe can rewrite this equation as:\n\nC = A + 4\n\nIn four years, Ben's age will be $\\frac{3}{5}$ of Amy's age at that time. This can be written as:\n\nB + 4 = $\\frac{3}{5}$ (A + 4)\n\nMultiplying both sides by 5 to eliminate the fraction, we get:\n\n5(B + 4) = 3(A + 4)\n\nExpanding the equation, we get:\n\n5B + 20 = 3A + 12\n\nSubtracting 20 from both sides, we get:\n\n5B = 3A - 8\n\nNow we have three equations:\n\n1) A + B + C = 18\n2) C = A + 4\n3) 5B = 3A - 8\n\nWe can substitute equation (2) into equation (1):\n\nA + B + (A + 4) = 18\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n2A + B = 14\n\nNow we have two equations:\n\n1) 2A + B = 14\n2) 5B = 3A - 8\n\nWe can solve for B in terms of A using equation (1):\n\nB = 14 - 2A\n\nSubstituting this expression for B into equation (2), we get:\n\n5(14 - 2A) = 3A - 8\n\nExpanding the equation, we get:\n\n70 - 10A = 3A - 8\n\nAdding 10A to both sides, we get:\n\n70 = 13A - 8\n\nAdding 8 to both sides, we get:\n\n78 = 13A\n\nDividing both sides by 13, we get:\n\nA = 6\n\nNow that we know Amy's age, we can find Chris's age using equation (2):\n\nC = A + 4\nC = 6 + 4\nC = 10\n\nTherefore, Chris is now 10 years old.\n\n\\boxed{10}", "decode_seconds": 23.866012642160058, "draft_calls": 0, "input_tokens": 114, "method": "native_ar", "output_hash": "384e51c8aee6e659eca901a2bdaec92df5cde6902760b20b1a67512b4e418d1c", "output_token_ids": [10267, 596, 79164, 29793, 596, 1510, 4325, 439, 362, 11, 7505, 596, 1510, 4325, 439, 426, 11, 323, 11517, 596, 1510, 4325, 439, 356, 382, 22818, 430, 279, 5578, 315, 872, 17051, 374, 220, 21, 11, 584, 649, 3350, 279, 24524, 1473, 4444, 489, 426, 489, 356, 8, 611, 220, 18, 284, 220, 21, 271, 58351, 6852, 2225, 11314, 555, 220, 18, 11, 584, 636, 1473, 32, 489, 426, 489, 356, 284, 220, 972, 271, 1687, 527, 1101, 2728, 430, 3116, 1667, 4227, 11, 11517, 574, 279, 1890, 4325, 439, 29793,